            <ArticleTitle>Two instruments to determine activated partial thromboplastin time: implications for heparin monitoring.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>383-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To measure the difference in therapeutic ranges of activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT) between two laboratory devices.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective, controlled laboratory study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University-affiliated hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirty inpatients receiving intravenous unfractionated heparin for treatment of myocardial infarction, unstable angina, deep venous thrombosis, or pulmonary embolism.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Therapeutic APTT ranges were determined by a portable (whole blood assay) and a central laboratory device (plasma assay) based on heparin serum concentrations. They were compared with APTT ranges equivalent to 1.5-2.5 times the mean normal determination.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The central laboratory and portable devices produced therapeutic ranges of 61-93 and 56-73 seconds, respectively. Both differed from conventional therapeutic ratios of 1.5-2.5 times the mean normal (41-68 sec). Mean absolute APTT differences between instruments were statistically significant (12 +/- 20 sec, p&lt;0.006), and 58% of paired APTT values differed by more than 10 seconds.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A fixed APTT ratio as a goal for monitoring unfractionated heparin may result in significant underanticoagulation. Individual therapeutic APTT ranges must be reported for each instrument if more than one is used for heparin monitoring.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
